        <p class="profile-detail-region">Life · Europe</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Otto von Bismarck</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">1815 CE – 1898 CE</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Born</span><span class="info-stat-val">1 April 1815 at Schönhausen, a family estate west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> in Prussian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>; son of Ferdinand von Bismarck, a Junker landowner and former officer, and Wilhelmine Mencken, daughter of a senior Berlin official</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Died</span><span class="info-stat-val">30 July 1898, just after midnight, at Friedrichsruh near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a>, aged 83, after years of failing health and gangrene in his foot</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Who they were</span><span class="info-stat-val">Member of the Prussian United Landtag (1847) and Landtag (1849); envoy to the Federal Diet at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> (1851–1859); ambassador to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> (1862); Minister President and Foreign Minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> (1862–1890); first Chancellor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a> (1871–1890); Count (1865), Prince (1871), Duke of Lauenburg (1890)</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">the 'iron and blood' speech of 1862; the wars against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the unification of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in 1871; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kulturkampf">Kulturkampf</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a>; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anti-socialist-laws">Anti-Socialist Laws</a>; sickness, accident and old-age insurance; the alliance system that kept the great powers at peace; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-berlin">Congress of Berlin</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin-conference">Berlin Conference</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanisation">Germanisation</a> policies against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a></span></div>
