    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Appointed minister president of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in 1862 to break a deadlock over the army budget, Bismarck governed for years without a legal budget and used three short wars, against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, to unite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> under Prussia by 1871.</li><li>As imperial chancellor he kept the new empire at peace through a web of alliances, while at home he fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a>, banned socialist organisations, pressed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanisation">Germanisation</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> and other minorities and created the first welfare state to win workers from socialism.</li><li>Dismissed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-ii">Wilhelm II</a> in 1890, he spent his last years attacking the emperor in his memoirs; historians praise his diplomacy but argue that his authoritarian system, with no check on the emperor, left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> a dangerous inheritance.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The only known recording of Bismarck's voice was made on an Edison <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phonograph">phonograph</a> in 1889; in it he quoted, among other things, the first verse of '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-marseillaise">La Marseillaise</a>', which a biographer took as a jibe at the French nineteen years after the war.</p></aside>
    </section>

