    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1815</span><span class="rf-text">Born at Schönhausen, a noble family estate west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1862</span><span class="rf-text">Appointed Minister President and Foreign Minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in September and gives his 'iron and blood' speech to the Budget Committee</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1864</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> defeat <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-schleswig-war">Second Schleswig War</a>, and Denmark gives up the duchies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schleswig">Schleswig</a> and Holstein</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1866</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> defeats <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> at Königgrätz in a seven-week war, and the German Confederation gives way to the North German Confederation</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1871</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-i">Wilhelm I</a> is proclaimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-emperor">German Emperor</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a> after the defeat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and Bismarck becomes the first Imperial Chancellor</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1878</span><span class="rf-text">Abandons the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kulturkampf">Kulturkampf</a> and, after two attempts on the emperor's life, has the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichstag">Reichstag</a> pass the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anti-socialist-laws">Anti-Socialist Laws</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1890</span><span class="rf-text">Resigns at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-ii">Wilhelm II</a>'s insistence on 18 March and is succeeded by Leo von Caprivi</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1898</span><span class="rf-text">Dies at Friedrichsruh, where he is entombed in the Bismarck Mausoleum</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="early">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Early Life</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Otto von Bismarck was born on 1 April 1815 at Schönhausen, a noble family estate west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> in Prussian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>. His father, Ferdinand von Bismarck, was a Junker landowner and former Prussian officer of modest means; his mother, Wilhelmine Mencken, was the well-educated daughter of a senior government official in Berlin, from a family of civil servants and academics. In 1816 the family moved to its Pomeranian estate, Kniephof, northeast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/szczecin">Stettin</a>, where he spent his childhood. Others saw him as a typical backwoods Junker, an image he encouraged by wearing uniform, but he was well educated and cosmopolitan, with a gift for conversation and a knowledge of English, French, Italian, Polish and Russian.</p><p>After school he studied law at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gottingen">Göttingen</a>, where he befriended the American John Lothrop Motley, and at Berlin from 1833 to 1835. He began training as a lawyer in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aachen">Aachen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potsdam">Potsdam</a> but soon resigned, having put his career at risk by taking unauthorised leave to pursue two English girls. After a shortened military service he ran the family estates at Schönhausen. Around the age of thirty he formed an intense friendship with Marie von Thadden-Trieglaff; a month after her death he asked for the hand of her cousin, Johanna von Puttkamer, a deeply religious woman, whom he married on 28 July 1847. They had three children, Marie, Herbert and Wilhelm.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Otto von Bismarck&#8221; cites where it covers Early years, Young politician, Ambassador to Russia and France:</p><ul><li>Otto Pflanze, Bismarck and the Development of Germany: The Period of Unification, 1815–1871 (Princeton University Press, 1971)</li><li>Edward Crankshaw, Bismarck (Viking Press, 1981)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rise">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Rise</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wilhelm-i.jpg" alt="Wilhelm I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-i">Wilhelm I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kaiser_Wilhelm_I_(3x4_cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wilhelm Kuntzemüller, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In 1847 Bismarck entered the new Prussian United Landtag, chosen through the ultra-conservative Gerlach brothers, and won a name as a royalist reactionary with a gift for stinging rhetoric who defended the king's divine right. In the revolution of 1848 he tried to raise the peasants of his estate to march on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> for the king. Elected to the Landtag in 1849, he then opposed German unification, fearing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> would lose its independence. From 1851 to 1859 he was Prussia's envoy to the Federal Diet in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a>, where he insisted on equal treatment with the Austrian envoy, fought a duel that ended without injury, and grew less reactionary and more pragmatic, convinced that Prussia must ally with other German states against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>. He then served as ambassador to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and, in 1862, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><p>In 1862 the liberal majority of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landtag-of-prussia">Prussian Landtag</a> refused to fund King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-i">Wilhelm I</a>'s reorganisation of the army, and the king, advised by Albrecht von Roon, appointed Bismarck Minister President and Foreign Minister on 23 September. Arguing that the constitution had a "legal loophole" where no budget was passed, he kept collecting taxes on the 1861 budget for four years, restricted the press and remained deeply unpopular; in 1863 a liberal coalition won over two-thirds of the seats. On 30 September 1862 he told the Budget Committee that the great questions of the time would be decided not by speeches and majority decisions "but by iron and blood".</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Otto von Bismarck&#8221; cites where it covers Young politician, Ambassador to Russia and France:</p><ul><li>Otto Pflanze, Bismarck and the Development of Germany: The Period of Unification, 1815–1871 (Princeton University Press, 1971)</li><li>Edward Crankshaw, Bismarck (Viking Press, 1981)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="deeds">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What They Did</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-berlin"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/congress-of-berlin.jpg" alt="Congress of Berlin" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-berlin">Congress of Berlin</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Congress_of_Berlin,_13_July_1878,_by_Anton_von_Werner.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Anton von Werner, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>With Roon's reorganised army and Moltke's strategy, Bismarck used three short wars to unify <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> without <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>. In 1864 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and Austria defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schleswig">Schleswig</a> and Holstein. In 1866 he provoked war with Austria over the duchies, allied secretly with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and, after the victory at Königgrätz, insisted on a soft peace with no annexations from Austria; the German Confederation was replaced by the Prussian-led North German Confederation. In 1870 he published an edited version of the Ems Dispatch to provoke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> into war. The South German states rallied to Prussia, the French armies were captured at Sedan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a>, and on 18 January 1871 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-i">Wilhelm I</a> was proclaimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-emperor">German Emperor</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a>. France ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a> and part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a>.</p><p>As the empire's first chancellor and Prussia's minister president, Bismarck controlled both foreign and domestic policy. Abroad, wanting no more territory, he isolated France and bound the other powers in alliances: the League of the Three Emperors, the Dual Alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> in 1879, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/triple-alliance-1882">Triple Alliance</a> in 1882 and the secret <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reinsurance-treaty">Reinsurance Treaty</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in 1887. He helped negotiate the settlement of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-berlin">Congress of Berlin</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-turkish-war-1877-1878">Russo-Turkish War</a> of 1877–1878 and organised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin-conference">Berlin Conference</a> of 1884–1885, as Germany acquired colonies from Togoland to German <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-guinea">New Guinea</a>. At home he gave Germany sickness insurance in 1883, accident insurance in 1884 and old-age and disability insurance in 1889, to tie workers to the state and draw them from the Social Democrats.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Otto von Bismarck&#8221; cites where it covers Minister President of Prussia, Blood and Iron speech, Defeat of Denmark, Defeat of Austria:</p><ul><li>Erich Eyck, Bismarck and the German Empire (W. W. Norton &amp; Company; 1st edition, 1964)</li><li>A.J.P. Taylor, Bismarck: the Man and the Statesman (Alfred A Knopf, 1955)</li><li>William L. Langer, European Alliances and Alignments, 1871–1890, 2nd Edition (New York, Knopf, 1964)</li><li>Edward Crankshaw, Bismarck (Viking Press, 1981)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="opponents">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Opponents &amp; Critics</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/anti-socialist-laws"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/anti-socialist-laws.jpg" alt="Anti-Socialist Laws" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/anti-socialist-laws">Anti-Socialist Laws</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Reichsgesetzblatt34_1878.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Bismarck's domestic rule was repressive. From 1871 his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kulturkampf">Kulturkampf</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a> imprisoned or exiled all Prussian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> and many priests, expelled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a>, banned most religious orders and cut off state payments to the clergy; the Catholics answered by building the Centre Party, and he abandoned the campaign in 1878. That year, blaming two attempts on the emperor's life on the socialists without evidence, he passed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anti-socialist-laws">Anti-Socialist Laws</a>, under which some 330 workers' organisations were banned, around 1,500 people imprisoned and nearly 900 expelled from their towns. His government pressed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanisation">Germanisation</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a>, the people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace-lorraine">Alsace-Lorraine</a> and above all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a>: Polish was pushed out of schools and administration, more than 30,000 people without German citizenship were expelled from the eastern provinces in 1885–1886, and a Settlement Commission bought estates for German farmers. In 1861 he had written privately of the Poles that "we have no choice but to wipe them out".</p><p>Critics then and since have judged him harshly. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a> called him "overbearing, violent, grasping, and unprincipled". Jonathan Steinberg describes a violent temper, intolerance of opposition and paranoia that made the Kulturkampf relentless, and quotes the British ambassador Odo Russell: "The demonic is stronger in him than in any man I know." Evans calls him "intimidating and unscrupulous". His giving in to the demand for Alsace-Lorraine, which turned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> into a permanent enemy, is often called his main mistake.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="last">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Last Years</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-ii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wilhelm-ii.jpg" alt="Wilhelm II" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-ii">Wilhelm II</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kaiser_Wilhelm_II_of_Germany_-_1902_(3x4_cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Studio of Thomas Heinrich Voigt, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-i">Wilhelm I</a> died in 1888 and his son Frederick III after only 99 days, leaving the throne to the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-ii">Wilhelm II</a>, who wanted to govern himself and to pursue rapid expansion. The two soon quarrelled. Bismarck tried to make the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anti-socialist-laws">Anti-Socialist Laws</a> permanent and, in an outburst, admitted he wanted the socialists to agitate until a violent clash gave a pretext to crush them; Wilhelm replied that he would not open his reign with a bloody campaign against his own subjects. Bismarck refused to sign a proclamation on the protection of workers and, when his coalition lost its majority, invited the Centre Party leader Ludwig Windthorst to talks without informing the emperor. After a furious argument he resigned at Wilhelm's insistence on 18 March 1890, aged 75, and was succeeded by Leo von Caprivi.</p><p>He was given the honorary rank of colonel-general with the dignity of field marshal and the title of Duke of Lauenburg, without the duchy, which he regarded as a mockery of his ambition. Elected to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichstag">Reichstag</a>, he never took his seat, humiliated at being forced to a second ballot by a Social Democrat. In resentful retirement at Friedrichsruh he wrote his memoirs, Thoughts and Memories, attacking Wilhelm II and presenting himself favourably, and published the secret <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reinsurance-treaty">Reinsurance Treaty</a>. After his wife's death in 1894 his health worsened; he refused treatment for gangrene in his foot, and died just after midnight on 30 July 1898. His sarcophagus calls him "A loyal German servant of Emperor Wilhelm I".</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Otto von Bismarck&#8221; cites where it covers Final years and forced resignation, Death:</p><ul><li>Gordon A. Craig, Germany, 1866–1945 (Oxford University Press, 1978)</li><li>Erich Eyck, Bismarck and the German Empire (W. W. Norton &amp; Company; 1st edition, 1964)</li><li>Norman Rich, Friedrich von Holstein: politics and diplomacy in the era of Bismarck and Wilhelm II (Cambridge University Press, 1965)</li><li>Katharine Lerman, Bismarck (Routledge, 2014)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/theodore-roosevelt"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/theodore-roosevelt.jpg" alt="Theodore Roosevelt" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/theodore-roosevelt">Theodore Roosevelt</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Theodore_Roosevelt_by_the_Pach_Bros_(4x5_cropped)_(3).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Adam Cuerden, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Bismarck's most durable achievement is the unification of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, which became one of the most powerful nations in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Jonathan Steinberg calls his achievements of 1862–1871 "the greatest diplomatic and political achievement by any leader in the last two centuries". Diplomatic historians praise his peace after 1871: A. J. P. Taylor called him "an honest broker of peace", <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eric-hobsbawm">Eric Hobsbawm</a> the "undisputed world champion at the game of multilateral diplomatic chess", and William L. Langer credits him with steering Europe through a dangerous period without serious conflict between the great powers. His welfare laws drew praise from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodore-roosevelt">Theodore Roosevelt</a> in 1916. His system did not survive him: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reinsurance-treaty">Reinsurance Treaty</a> lapsed after 1890, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-ii">Wilhelm II</a>'s Weltpolitik united the other powers against Germany before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>.</p><p>Others see a darker inheritance. Steinberg, Evans, Taylor and Palmer regard him as an ambivalent figure who left no system for less skilled successors, and by allowing no effective constitutional check on the emperor he placed a time bomb in the foundation of the state he created. Henry Kissinger argues that he so undermined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberalism">liberalism</a> that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar-republic">Weimar Republic</a> could never make it succeed. In imperial Germany nationalists built monuments and Bismarck towers to him, while Catholics, Social Democrats and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> remembered him with hostility. Robert Gerwarth shows how the Bismarck myth, built up after his death, became a weapon against the Weimar Republic.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Otto von Bismarck&#8221; cites where it covers Memory and myth, Place names, Honours:</p><ul><li>Edgar Feuchtwanger, Bismarck (Routledge, 2002)</li><li>Robert Gerwarth, The Bismarck Myth: Weimar Germany and the Legacy of the Iron Chancellor (Clarendon Press, 2005)</li><li>Ludwig von Arndt, Die Orden und Ehrenzeichen des Reichskanzlers Fürst Otto von Bismarck (Phaleristischer Verlag, 2008)</li></ul></details><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Songs in history</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/songs/die-wacht-am-rhein">Die Wacht am Rhein<span>1840</span></a><a href="/songs/rota">Rota<span>1908</span></a></p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Otto von Bismarck, a Prussian Junker and minister president from 1862, unified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> through wars against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and served as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a>'s first chancellor until 1890. He kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>'s great powers at peace and created the first welfare state, while fighting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a>, the socialists and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a>, and leaving an authoritarian state that historians still debate.</em></p>

